Package: wforce
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, wforce-common (= ${binary:Version}), wforce-lua-dist
Description: Daemon for detecting brute force attacks
 The goal of 'wforce' is to detect brute forcing of passwords across many
 servers, services and instances. In order to support the real world, brute
 force detection policy can be tailored to deal with "bulk, but legitimate"
 users of your service, as well as botnet-wide slowscans of passwords.
 The aim is to support the largest of installations, providing services to
 hundreds of millions of users.
 .
 This package provides the actual daemon and its client command.

Package: wforce-trackalert
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, wforce (= ${binary:Version}), wforce-lua-dist
Description: Longterm abuse data reporting and alerter
 Trackalert is designed to be an optional service to complement
 wforce. Whereas wforce provides a toolkit to combat abuse of
 logins such as password brute forcing in realtime, trackalert is
 designed to look at abuse asynchronously, using long-term report data
 stored in an external DB such as elasticsearch, and to send alerts on
 potential login abuse.

Package: wforce-replfwd
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, wforce-common (= ${binary:Version})
Description: Daemon for forwarding wforce replication events
 The goal of 'replfwd' is to forward wforce replication
 messages between clusters of wforce servers. It does this
 by acting as a cluster member (or Sibling) and forwarding
 received events to a set of configured Forwarding destinations.
 It also receives replication events from a set of configured
 Forwarding sources, and sends those events to the local
 cluster members.

Package: wforce-common
Architecture: any
Breaks: wforce (<< ${binary:Version})
Replaces: wforce (<< ${binary:Version})
Depends: adduser, bsdextrautils, ucf, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Common files for all wforce packages
 The goal of 'wforce' is to detect brute forcing of passwords across many
 servers, services and instances. In order to support the real world, brute
 force detection policy can be tailored to deal with "bulk, but legitimate"
 users of your service, as well as botnet-wide slowscans of passwords.
 The aim is to support the largest of installations, providing services to
 hundreds of millions of users.
 .
 This package provides the shared configuration files and system account
 for the wforce daemons.
